# Shell scripts must keep LF endings in the working tree.
#
# Git for Windows sets core.autocrlf=true by default, so without this rule a
# Windows clone checks run_all.sh out with CRLF. WSL then cannot run it: the
# shebang line ends in a carriage return, bash reports
#
#     ./run_all.sh: cannot execute: required file not found
#
# and `bash run_all.sh` fails later with a syntax error on the first loop. The
# files are stored with LF, so this concerns the checkout only.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The private RTL copies of three integration packages are built by applying a
# patch file to a copy of the design (`patch -p1 < <package>/<name>.patch`).
# Checked out with CRLF, the patch no longer matches the LF design files, so
# patch files keep LF in the working tree for the same reason.
*.patch text eol=lf
